# Day 02: Running Local AI on an AMD GPU (RX 9070 XT)


Part 1 ran models on mini PCs (CPU only). This episode moves to a gaming PC with a real GPU. It covers why model architecture matters for VRAM, how to find smaller quants, and how speculative decoding took generation from ~32 to ~39 tokens/sec.

---

## Hardware

| Component | Spec |
|---|---|
| GPU | AMD Radeon RX 9070 XT (16GB VRAM) |
| CPU | AMD Ryzen 7 9800X3D |
| RAM | 64GB DDR5 |
| Software | LM Studio, Vulkan backend |

No Nvidia card and no CUDA. LM Studio's Vulkan backend (llama.cpp under the hood) runs fine on AMD.

---

## Dense vs MoE

| | Dense (Qwen3.8 27B) | MoE (Qwen 3.6 35B-A3B) |
|---|---|---|
| Parameters used per token | All 27B | ~3B active |
| Needs to fit in VRAM? | Yes, or it slows down badly | Tolerates spilling into system RAM |
| Best fit for | A GPU with enough VRAM | Big system RAM, smaller GPU |

- **Dense models** read every weight for every token. If part of the model sits in system RAM, each token waits on that slower memory.
- **Mixture-of-Experts (MoE)** models only activate a few "experts" per token. The `A3B` in the name means about 3B active parameters. Less data moves per token, so offloading to system RAM hurts much less.

With 16GB of VRAM, I went with the dense 27B and picked a quant small enough to fit on the GPU.

---

## Finding the right quant

LM Studio's **Discover** tab often shows only a few staff-pick quants (e.g. Q4_K_M, Q6_K, Q8_0), and those may not fit in 16GB.

**Fix:** search for the Hugging Face repo name directly in LM Studio's search bar, or paste the full HF URL. For example:

```
unsloth/Qwen3.8-27B-GGUF
```

That shows the full list of download options (Q3_K_M, IQ4_XS, UD-Q3/Q4 variants, etc.).

**Quant I used:** `Q3` (~14 GB)

---

## Speculative decoding (the big speedup)

Speculative decoding has a fast "draft" step guess the next few tokens. The main model then checks all of them at once. When the guesses are right, you get several tokens for the price of one pass.

The more draft tokens, the more guesses get thrown away when they're wrong. So more is **not** always better:

| Draft tokens | Tokens/sec |
|---|---|
| Off (baseline) | ~32.5 |
| 1 | ~35.5 |
| **2** | **~37–39** ✅ |
| 3 | ~25.8 ❌ |

**Result:** 2 draft tokens gave about a 20% speedup over baseline. Setting it to 3 was actually slower than having it off.

